From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] viogpu_umd: D3D12 video decoding on Vulkan Video

D3D12 video decoding (the video DDI's decode tables) for H.264, HEVC Main/Main
10, VP9 profiles 0/2 and AV1 Main 8/10-bit, on the Vulkan device's video
decode queue (NVDEC on NVIDIA hosts, through Venus). ffmpeg's d3d12va decodes
bit-exact against libavcodec on every test clip but one (below); decoding and
encoding chain on the GPU (h264 d3d12va -> hevc_d3d12va, 720p at ~10x).

vkd3d-proton:
- the video decode queue family and VK_KHR_video_decode_queue, decode_h264,
  _h265, _av1, _vp9 (and VP9's feature) when the device has them;
- buffers get VIDEO_DECODE_SRC (upload heaps included), profile-independent,
  their Vulkan buffer padded to 4 KiB: decoders read bitstreams in aligned
  sizes, applications size buffers to the byte;
- NV12/P010 textures (P010 is G16 here) are concurrent with the video
  families, so the decoder can copy its output into them on the decode queue;
- vkd3d_command_list_track_interop_resource(): a texture that Vulkan commands
  recorded through the DXVK interop use still gets the initial layout
  transition before the list (a decoder output's first use otherwise lost its
  luma to the transition).

viogpu_umd:
- umd_video.h: what encoding and decoding share (the device's Vulkan side;
  encoding and decoding each where the device has them); lists, allocators and
  queues per video family;
- umd_video_decode.cpp: caps (profiles and formats as far as the host decodes
  them, tier 2), decoders and heaps (the heap owns the session and the DPB: an
  image per slot, decoded into, DPB and output coinciding), DecodeFrame:
  - parameter sets rebuilt from DXVA's picture parameters (H.264 SPS/PPS,
    HEVC VPS/SPS/PPS, the AV1 sequence header; scaling lists in the PPS), new
    session parameters when they change;
  - references by the application's textures -> DPB slots; AV1's order hints,
    sign biases, saved order hints and skip mode frames derived as the spec
    does from what each slot keeps;
  - the picture copied into the application's output texture (both planes);
- umd_dxva.h: DXVA's structures (the D3D10/11 decoder's definitions);
- NV12/P010 report decoder output support (the runtime checks it).

Known limits: progressive H.264 only; no AV1 film grain (it needs an output
picture apart from the DPB); DXVA has no AV1 refresh_frame_flags, which
NVIDIA's decoder uses: with 0xff, a multi-tile 8-bit AV1 test clip decodes
with off-by-one U samples here and there (~75 dB), everything else exact.
